            <ArticleTitle>Tonsillectomy techniques and pain: a review of randomized controlled trials and call for standardization.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>364-70</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVES" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To review the literature comparing operative techniques in tonsillectomy and postoperative pain.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Randomized controlled trials comparing operative techniques and pain outcomes over a 10-year period were reviewed.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">44 trials met inclusion criteria for analysis. A total of nine different techniques for complete or partial tonsillectomy were compared. There were several studies with contradictory results. Many of the studies were performed under different parameters.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Future studies comparing tonsillectomy technique and pain outcomes should be conducted using a standard protocol to allow for accurate comparisons between numerous techniques.</AbstractText>
                <CopyrightInformation>(c) 2007 S. Karger AG, Basel.</CopyrightInformation>
            </Abstract>
